title: Language Overview
files:
  - em.core:em.utils/SoftUart.em.ts

steps:

  - cmds:
      - open 1
    focus: [1,1]
    text: |

      {[ht,$start]EM&bull;Script Language Overview}

      If not already obvious, **EM&bull;Script** ≡ **TypeScript**&thinsp;!!

      Using *progrmming conventions* reinenforced visually by **EM&bull;Browser**, **TypeScript** effectively "hosts" **EM&bull;Script**.

      But why **TypeScript** [**TS**] &ndash; especially when targeting embed&shy;ded HW typically programmed in C&thinsp;??

      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} start with a stable, widely-deployed language&thinsp;....<br>
      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} leverage its robust support within **VS Code**&thinsp;....<br>
      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} add a novel back-end program translator&thinsp;....<br>
      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} generate highly-optimized target C/C++&thinsp;....

      Starting with {[cu]SoftUart}, you'll learn key **EM&bull;Script** con&shy;structs &ndash; but seen on the **TypeScript** language surface.

  - cmds:
      - mark 1 A 13
      - mark 1 B 19
    focus: [1,1]
    text: |

      {[ht,splitscreen]One Unit &ndash; Two Domains}

      The {[cx]em.utils/SoftUart.em.ts} **Unit** implements a low-level (albeit portable) *bit-banged* serial transmitter.

      When running on target HW, the {[cf]put} function serializes a single byte of data over some physical GPIO pin.
  
      If used by an **EM&bull;Script** application, however, this **Unit** executes within <i><u>two</u></i> distinct programming domains:

      {[bi,counter_1]} *target* domain, where functions like {[cf]put} will run<br>
      {[bi,counter_2]} *meta* domain, where app configuration takes place

      Unlike resource-limited domain {[bi,counter_1]}, the code of domain {[bi,counter_2]} runs inside the **EM&bull;Script** translator &ndash; on your PC.

      The **TS** namespace at {[bm,A]} contains all of the *meta* code; the special comment at {[bm,B]} begins the *target* code.

  - focus: [1,1]
    text: |

      {[ht,timeline]Lifecycle Functions}

      The {[cu]SoftUart} **Unit** implements two *intrinsic* functions called during the **EM&bull;Script** program lifecycle:

      {[cs]em$construct} [**`meta`**]&thinsp; called to initialize private state<br>
      {[cs]em$startup&nbsp;&nbsp;} [**`targ`**]&thinsp; called before program {[cs]em$run}

      {[in]Programs like ⟪[cu]BlinkerP⟫ viewed in earlier tours imple&shy;ment the ⟪[cs]em$run⟫ intrinsic as their main entry-point.}

      The **EM&bull;Script** translator sequences intrinsic function calls &ndash; at build-time [**`meta`**] as well as run-time [**`targ`**].

      By design, each intrinsic belongs to only *one* **EM&bull;Script** domain &ndash; and may apply to just some **Unit** *flavors*.